> - what is rate, period time, period size, period, tick
> time  in *hw_params* means ? and what is used for ?

hw_params are the essential settings for performing PCM transfer.
At least, rate, format, channels, period_size (or time), buffer_size
(time, or nperiods) must be defined.

rate = sample frequency, usually in Hz.

period = the period of interrupts DMA generates.  Usually, a hardware
buffer is set up as a certain number of (continuous) data chunks with
equal sizes defined as period_size.  (i.e., buffer_size = nperiods *
period_size.)  When the hardware processes period_size samples, it
should generate an IRQ.

ALSA introduces the term "frame" to indicate the unit size to
determine buffer and period sizes.
1 frame is defined as sample_bytes * channels.  For example, with
2-channel 16bit samples, 1 frame corresponds to 4 bytes.

You don't need tick_time.  Forget it :)


> - what is xfer_align, start treshold, stop treshold,
> silence treshold in *sw_params* mean ?
>   and what is used for ?

sw_params are additional optional settings.  Skip this at first.
If you face a problem that needs special tuning (e.g. delaying the DMA
start, ignoring XRUN, etc.), check this again.


> - if i want to delay input (capture) and output
> (playback) for a milisecond what function must i used
> ? can both of delay (I/O) be different ?

No special ALSA API for delay lines.  You need to do it by yourself.

If you mean to program a low-latency full-duplex audio, you'd better
to check JACK rather than writing directly with ALSA API.


